chem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/Barbexaclone","https://www.aifa.gov.it/-/comunicazione-sul-medicinale-a-denominazione-generica-barbesaclone-25-mg-e-100-mg-compresse-rivestite-22-05-2014-","https://www.swissmedic.ch/dam/swissmedic/en/dokumente/stab/journal/swissmedic_journal072006.pdf.download.pdf/swissmedic_journal072006.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Maliasin","field":"trade_names","proposed_value":"Maliasin","rationale":"Maliasin is listed as a synonym/trade name in PubChem and as the Swiss product name in Swissmedic/Swiss product information.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/Barbexaclone","https://ch.oddb.org/de/gcc/fachinfo/reg/31862","https://www.swissmedic.ch/dam/swissmedic/en/dokumente/stab/journal/swissmedic_journal072006.pdf.download.pdf/swissmedic_journal072006.pdf"]},{"current_value":"No - not available in U.S. for seizure control; historical Swiss/Italian European documentation identified.","field":"available_in_us","proposed_value":"No - no FDA-approved U.S. label/application identified; non-U.S. Swiss and Italian product/access documentation identified.","rationale":"FDA/Drugs@FDA is the appropriate U.S. approval source; no FDA label/application was identified for the searched aliases. Swiss and AIFA sources document non-U.S. product/access history.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-approvals-and-databases/about-drugsfda","https://open.fda.gov/apis/drug/label/example-api-queries/","https://www.swissmedic.ch/dam/swissmedic/en/dokumente/stab/journal/swissmedic_journal072006.pdf.download.pdf/swissmedic_journal072006.pdf","https://www.aifa.gov.it/-/comunicazione-sul-medicinale-a-denominazione-generica-barbesaclone-25-mg-e-100-mg-compresse-rivestite-22-05-2014-"]},{"current_value":"Coated tablet/dragee","field":"formulations_available","proposed_value":"Coated tablet/dragee","rationale":"Swiss information lists Maliasin 25 mg and 100 mg dragees; AIFA refers to Barbesaclone 25 mg and 100 mg coated tablets.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://ch.oddb.org/de/gcc/fachinfo/reg/31862","https://www.aifa.gov.it/-/comunicazione-sul-medicinale-a-denominazione-generica-barbesaclone-25-mg-e-100-mg-compresse-rivestite-22-05-2014-"]},{"current_value":"Generalized tonic-clonic; Absence; Myoclonic-atonic; Myoclonic; Lennox-Gastaut syndrome; Focal; Infantile spasms/BNS","field":"epilepsy_type","proposed_value":"Generalized tonic-clonic; Absence; Myoclonic-atonic; Myoclonic; Lennox-Gastaut syndrome; Focal; Infantile spasms/BNS","rationale":"Swiss product information lists Grand mal epilepsy, petit mal/absence prophylaxis, myoclonic-astatic petit mal including Lennox syndrome, impulsive myoclonic petit mal, Jackson and other focal epilepsies, and BNS spasms.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://ch.oddb.org/de/gcc/fachinfo/reg/31862"]},{"current_value":"Swiss Fachinformation: adults usually 200-400 mg/day; school-age children 50-300 mg/day; individualized titration from 100 mg/day in adults.","field":"typical_doses_per_day","proposed_value":"Swiss Fachinformation: adults usually 200-400 mg/day; school-age children 50-300 mg/day; individualized titration from 100 mg/day in adults.","rationale":"The dose ranges and adult titration start are directly supported by the Swiss product information.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://ch.oddb.org/de/gcc/fachinfo/reg/31862"]},{"current_value":"No explicit fixed maximum in Swiss Fachinformation; average adult daily dose 200-400 mg/day and pediatric 50-300 mg/day.","field":"maximum_approved_daily_dose","proposed_value":"No explicit fixed maximum in Swiss Fachinformation; average adult daily dose 200-400 mg/day and school-age pediatric dose 50-300 mg/day.","rationale":"The Swiss source gives average dose ranges but does not state a fixed maximum daily dose.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://ch.oddb.org/de/gcc/fachinfo/reg/31862"]},{"current_value":"Swiss Fachinformation: adult titration begins at 100 mg/day; children may titrate slowly using 25 mg tablets.","field":"minimum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"No explicit minimum effective dose stated; Swiss Fachinformation dosing starts adults at 100 mg/day and titrates every 2 days to 1 week; children may be titrated slowly using 25 mg dragees/tablets.","rationale":"A starting titration dose is not the same as an evidence-based minimum effective dose, so the cell should be worded more cautiously.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://ch.oddb.org/de/gcc/fachinfo/reg/31862"]},{"current_value":"Phenobarbital and L-propylhexedrine salt; Swiss Fachinformation attributes antiseizure effect to phenobarbital-related barbiturate reduction of neuronal excitability and inhibition of pathologic CNS excitation, with propylhexedrine intended to counter unwanted sedation.","field":"mechanism_of_action","proposed_value":"Phenobarbital and L-propylhexedrine salt; Swiss Fachinformation attributes antiseizure effect to phenobarbital-related barbiturate reduction of neuronal excitability and inhibition of pathologic CNS excitation, with propylhexedrine intended to counter unwanted sedation.","rationale":"Swiss product information and PubMed/PubChem sources support that barbexaclone is a phenobarbital-propylhexedrine salt; Swiss product information supports the stated mechanism wording.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://ch.oddb.org/de/gcc/fachinfo/reg/31862","https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/Barbexaclone","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23981808/"]},{"current_value":"Moderate","field":"mechanism_confidence","proposed_value":"Moderate","rationale":"A non-U.S. product information source supports the mechanism, but controlled clinical efficacy evidence for barbexaclone is limited, making moderate confidence reasonable.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://ch.oddb.org/de/gcc/fachinfo/reg/31862","https://www.ilae.org/files/ilaeGuideline/Guidelines.pdf"]},{"current_value":"EMA/UK SmPC","field":"mechanism_source_tier","proposed_value":"Swiss Fachinformation / non-U.S. product information (not EMA/UK SmPC)","rationale":"The cited mechanism source is a Swiss Fachinformation page via ODDB, not an EMA EPAR, EMA SmPC, or UK eMC/SmPC.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://ch.oddb.org/de/gcc/fachinfo/reg/31862"]},{"current_value":"Enzyme inducer: phenobarbital component induces hepatic microsomal enzymes/P450 with broad interaction potential.","field":"enzyme_inducing_or_inhibiting","proposed_value":"Enzyme inducer: phenobarbital component induces hepatic microsomal enzymes/P450 with broad interaction potential.","rationale":"Swiss product information states long-term phenobarbital use induces microsomal liver enzymes/cytochrome P450 and can accelerate metabolism of substrates.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://ch.oddb.org/de/gcc/fachinfo/reg/31862"]},{"current_value":"Liver metabolism of phenobarbital component with renal elimination; propylhexedrine oxidative metabolism described","field":"major_organ_for_metabolism","proposed_value":"Liver metabolism of phenobarbital component with renal elimination; propylhexedrine oxidative metabolism described","rationale":"Swiss product information states phenobarbital is metabolized to p-hydroxy derivative/conjugates, a fraction appears unchanged in urine, phenobarbital is renally eliminated, and L-propylhexedrine undergoes oxidative metabolism.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://ch.oddb.org/de/gcc/fachinfo/reg/31862","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/3709618/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6139756/"]},{"current_value":"Phenobarbital component 2-4 days in adults; 3 days in children; 3-7 days in neonates; 4-8 days with cirrhosis","field":"half_life_range","proposed_value":"Phenobarbital component 2-4 days in adults; 3 days in children; 3-7 days in neonates; 4-8 days with cirrhosis","rationale":"Swiss product information supports these phenobarbital half-life ranges for special populations.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://ch.oddb.org/de/gcc/fachinfo/reg/31862"]},{"current_value":"Swiss Fachinformation frequency categories: frequent 1-10% not itemized for all symptoms; occasional 0.1-1% calcium metabolism disorder and weight loss; rare 0.01-0.1% aplastic/megaloblastic anemia, pancytopenia, thrombocytopenia, leukopenia, agranulocytosis, allergic/respiratory hypersensitivity, hallucinations, hemorrhage, constipation, vomiting, gingival hyperplasia, SJS/TEN, exfoliative dermatitis, rash; frequency not reliably estimable 0% exact percentage unavailable for somnolence, ataxia, nystagmus, dizziness, respiratory depression, fatigue.","field":"adverse_symptoms_percentages","proposed_value":"Swiss Fachinformation frequency categories: frequent >1/100 to <=1/10, occasional >1/1000 to <=1/100, rare >1/10000 to <=1/1000. Occasional: calcium metabolism disorders, weight loss. Rare: aplastic anemia, megaloblastic anemia, pancytopenia, thrombocytopenia, leukopenia, agranulocytosis, allergic reactions/skin and respiratory hypersensitivity, systemic lupus erythematosus, optical hallucinations, hemorrhage, constipation, vomiting, gingival hyperplasia, erythema multiforme, Stevens-Johnson syndrome, Lyell syndrome/TEN, exfoliative dermatitis, nonspecific drug rash, photosensitization, urticaria. Frequency not reliably estimable: restlessness, drowsiness, ataxia, nystagmus, dizziness, respiratory depression, hepatitis, jaundice, increased GGT, irritability, fatigue, high fever.","rationale":"The Swiss source supports the frequency categories and listed events, but the row's phrase '0% exact percentage unavailable' is inaccurate because unestimable frequency is not 0%. The proposed text also captures additional listed events and avoids overstating exact percentages.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://ch.oddb.org/de/gcc/fachinfo/reg/31862"]},{"current_value":"No QT interval effect described in Swiss Fachinformation; contraindications/cautions include tachyarrhythmias, severe angina, severe myocardial damage.","field":"qt_interval_effect","proposed_value":"QT effect not characterized in located product information; Swiss Fachinformation does not describe a QT-interval effect but lists contraindications/cautions including tachyarrhythmias, severe angina pectoris, and severe myocardial damage.","rationale":"The Swiss source supports the cardiac contraindications/cautions. Absence of QT wording in the source should not be treated as evidence of no QT effect.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"insufficient_evidence","supporting_sources":["https://ch.oddb.org/de/gcc/fachinfo/reg/31862"]},{"current_value":"N/A","field":"pubmed_phase_ii_iii_rct_links","proposed_value":"N/A","rationale":"No qualifying phase II/III placebo-controlled randomized epilepsy RCT link was found. PubMed PMID 14606 is an open clinical assay, not placebo-controlled; ILAE evidence review lists no/inadequate data for barbexaclone in several monotherapy categories.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/14606/","https://www.ilae.org/files/ilaeGuideline/Guidelines.pdf","https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/Barbexaclone"]},{"current_value":"No qualifying phase II/III placebo-controlled randomized epilepsy RCT was retained in the PubMed RCT audit/gap review as of 05-20-2026; RCT section set to N/A per current scope.","field":"rct_pubmed_verification_notes","proposed_value":"No qualifying phase II/III placebo-controlled randomized epilepsy RCT was identified as of 05-20-2026. PubMed PMID 14606 is an open clinical assay and does not qualify; ILAE evidence review found no/inadequate efficacy/effectiveness data for barbexaclone in reviewed epilepsy categories.","rationale":"The existing conclusion is correct, but the proposed text documents why the visible PubMed clinical-trial record does not qualify.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/14606/","https://www.ilae.org/files/ilaeGuideline/Guidelines.pdf"]},{"current_value":"barbesaclone; barbexaclonum; Maliasin","field":"pubmed_search_aliases","proposed_value":"barbesaclone; barbexaclonum; barbexaclon; barbexaclona; Maliasin; CHP phenobarbital; phenobarbital propylhexedrine salt","rationale":"PubChem lists additional aliases and MeSH entry terms that are useful for avoiding duplicate rows and improving literature searches.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"missing_source","supporting_sources":["https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/Barbexaclone"]},{"current_value":"Not FDA-cleared; Swissmedic Journal documents Maliasin authorization no. 31862 as an antiepileptic, and AIFA Barbesaclone 25 mg/100 mg distribution communications are documented.","field":"year_fda_cleared","proposed_value":"Not FDA-cleared/approved; no FDA label/application identified. Swissmedic Journal documents Maliasin authorization no. 31862 as an antiepileptic, and AIFA documents Barbesaclone coated-tablet access/distribution communications.","rationale":"The field is substantively correct. The proposed text uses FDA approval terminology and avoids implying a cleared year exists.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-approvals-and-databases/about-drugsfda","https://www.swissmedic.ch/dam/swissmedic/en/dokumente/stab/journal/swissmedic_journal072006.pdf.downl